      "latency_ms": 7031.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 556,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nTo truly understand what water is, we can look at it through a few different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is a molecule made up of three atoms: **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**. This is why its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**. \n* **The \"Magnet\" Effect:** Water molecules have a unique shape—like a tiny Mickey Mouse head—which makes one side slightly positive (the hydrogens) and the other side slightly negative (the oxygen). Because of this, water molecules love to stick to each other and to other substances. This property makes water an incredible \"universal solvent,\" meaning it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid.\n\n### 2. The States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct forms depending on the temperature:\n* **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Uniquely, solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water heats up and evaporates or boils at 100°C (212°F).\n\n### 3. Its Role on Earth\nWater dominates our planet. \n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth’s surface** is covered in water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a closed loop. It evaporates from the oceans into the clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow (precipitation), flows through rivers and groundwater (collection), and evaporates again. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 4. Its Role in Life\nWater is the biological medium of life. \n* **The Human Body:** The adult human body is made up of about **60% water**. \n* **Survival:** While humans can survive for weeks without food, we can only survive about 3 to 5 days without water. It regulates our body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and transports nutrients through our blood.\n\n**In short:** Water is the fundamental elixir of our planet—a simple chemical combination that makes life, weather, and geography possible."
